## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

Part 3.3 is the **third and final beat of the 3-part programming sub-arc** (3.1 concepts → 3.2 rules + building blocks → **3.3 templates**). Everything before this was theory under tension: 3.1 gave the economic model (stimulus / fatigue / MRV / biasing), 3.2 gave the standing orders and the assembly architecture (24 atoms → 6 compound patterns → variations → the Variation Cycle). 3.3 is the article where the abstract machine is **finally switched on and shown running** — two complete, literal, week-by-week programs a reader can type into Hevy on Monday and execute.

The article's whole structure is a controlled comparison. It presents **two templates that are different configurations of the same machine, producing the same output:**

1. **Template 1 — Anterior / Posterior split (AP).** 4 days/week, ~75 min/session, each muscle hit **2× per week.** Mon Anterior / Tue Posterior / Wed rest / Thu Anterior-variation / Fri Posterior-variation / Sat–Sun rest. ~19 working sets per session. The "workhorse split for transformation work."
2. **Template 2 — Full Body Every Other Day (FBEOD).** 3–4 days/week, ~60–70 min/session, each muscle hit **3× per week.** Mon quad-dominant / Wed hinge-dominant / Fri mixed. ~15 working sets per session. Volume distributed across more days, less per-session damage, MPS elevated more consistently.

The article is emphatic on the punchline: **==Choose based on schedule, recovery capacity, and personal preference — not because one is "better."==** Both land each muscle in the **12–18 sets/muscle/week** MAV-to-MRV band (the exact operating zone the 3.1 poster's hero curve was built around). The differences are *frequency* and *session length*, not *goal*.

But there is a second, equally important idea braided through the piece, and a generic "here are two workout charts" poster would miss it entirely: **the program runs on two clocks.** The *weekly clock* is the split (the two templates). The *mesocycle clock* is the **progression cycle** — over months, each exercise slot rotates **isolation/machine → compound/free-weight → back to isolation at a higher ceiling** (the vertical-push slot: Machine Shoulder Press → Seated DB Press → Standing OHP → back to Machine at a higher load, 8 weeks each). This is a specific instance of 3.2's Variation Cycle, *pre-built* into the templates so the reader doesn't have to design it. The article's stable-vs-variable maxim seals it: **==the principles are stable; the exercise choices are variables.==** The pattern slot (vertical push) is fixed forever; the exercise filling it rotates across mesocycles.

The prior posters used distinct sub-archetypes inside the locked Engineering Diagnostic Instrument style: Part 1.1/1.2 were **lookup matrices**, Part 2.0 a **signal-trace day-strip**, Part 3.1 an **operating-band curve dashboard with a fault annex**, Part 3.2 a **build-up assembly schematic with a standing-orders rail.** Part 3.3 needs another distinct-but-cohesive sub-archetype. The cleanest fit for an article that is fundamentally *a controlled comparison of two configurations of one machine, plus a long-clock progression timeline*: a **dual-configuration datasheet** — the kind of engineering spec sheet that shows two variant configurations of the same product side by side (two engine layouts, two PSU models), each with its own **weekly cadence / timing diagram**, both converging on **one shared output readout** (the per-muscle volume meter landing in the MAV→MRV band), with a **mesocycle progression timeline** running underneath as the long clock and a **configuration selector** that routes the reader to A or B by schedule and recovery.

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** *These are not two competing workouts to argue about. They are two configurations of one machine, tuned for different schedules and recovery budgets, and they produce the identical output. Pick the configuration that fits your week, run it unchanged for 8–12 weeks, and rotate the exercise inside each slot across mesocycles — not the slot itself.* The visual must make "same output, different cadence" the dominant read, and make the two clocks (weekly split vs. monthly slot-rotation) legible as two separate timekeeping systems that both have to be running.

