## 1. The Big Idea

_Two runnable programs are shown as two configurations of the same machine: the **AP split** (4 days, 2× frequency, ~19 sets/session) and **FBEOD** (3 days, 3× frequency, ~15 sets/session). Different weekly cadence, identical output — both drive every muscle into the same 12–18 set/week MAV→MRV band. A configuration selector routes the reader by schedule + recovery, and a mesocycle progression timeline underneath shows the one thing that changes over months: the exercise inside each slot rotates isolation → compound → isolation-at-a-higher-ceiling, while the slot itself never moves._ The poster is a two-configuration engineering datasheet a lifter pins beside the rack — pick a column, read its week, execute for a mesocycle, rotate the slots, repeat.

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure

A **dual-configuration datasheet with a shared output readout and a mesocycle timeline.** The visual language is the engineering spec sheet that documents two variants of one product: two parallel **configuration columns**, each with its own weekly **timing diagram** (a 7-day cadence strip), feeding a single **output meter** that proves the two variants are spec'd to the same target. Beneath both runs a **long-clock timeline** showing how the machine evolves across months, and a small **selector gate** tells you which configuration to build.

The composition has four functional zones:

- **Zone 1 — The two configuration columns (hero, side by side).** Left column = **CONFIG A · ANTERIOR/POSTERIOR**, right column = **CONFIG B · FULL-BODY EOD**. Each column is a self-contained datasheet block: a **spec header** (days/week, session length, frequency-per-muscle, sets/session), a **weekly cadence strip** (a horizontal 7-cell Mon–Sun timing diagram with session cells filled and rest cells empty/ghosted), and a **compact session readout** showing the day-templates as dense mono exercise rows. The two columns are deliberately the same width and the same vertical rhythm — visual equality is the argument ("neither is better").
- **Zone 2 — The shared output readout (the convergence).** Both columns funnel — via two thin converging rails — into **one** horizontal **volume meter** sitting between/below them: a per-muscle weekly-set bar chart with the **MAV→MRV operating band shaded** (12–18 sets/muscle/week), with both Config A and Config B plotted as paired bars per muscle (Quads · Hams · Chest · Back · Shoulders · Arms) landing *inside the same band*. This is the literal proof of "same goal, different route," and it's a direct callback to the Part 3.1 operating-band curve so the pillar reads continuously.
- **Zone 3 — The mesocycle progression timeline (the long clock).** A full-width horizontal timeline beneath the output readout: four mesocycle segments (Meso 1 → 4, 8 weeks each) with the **vertical-push slot** drawn as a worked rotation chain across them — `MACHINE SHOULDER PRESS → SEATED DB PRESS → STANDING OHP → MACHINE PRESS (higher ceiling)` — annotated to show the isolation→compound→isolation arc and the rising load ceiling. A fixed "SLOT" label rail above the chain makes the point that the *slot stays put* while the *exercise rotates*.
- **Zone 4 — The configuration selector + adaptation strip (the operator controls).** A small **decision gate** (schedule + recovery → A or B) with the article's when-to-use / when-to-avoid contrasts as two short routing rules, and a bottom **adaptation strip** of the five "adapt, don't rebuild" levers rendered as instrument-panel adjustment chips.

This sub-archetype — **dual-configuration datasheet + shared output meter + mesocycle timeline + selector gate** — is deliberately different from Part 2.0's single signal trace, Part 3.1's single hero curve, and Part 3.2's vertical build-up, while remaining unmistakably "Engineering Diagnostic Instrument." Where 3.2 showed *how the machine is assembled*, 3.3 shows *the assembled machine running in two configurations across time.*

